+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a DST the same as a 1031 exchange?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. A 1031 exchange is the tax rule. A DST share is one kind of property you can buy with it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a DST a REIT?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. A REIT is a company, and its owners hold shares in it. A DST share counts, for federal income tax, as owning part of the trust's real estate itself. Because of that, it can go into a 1031 exchange."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is income from a DST taxed?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For federal income tax, you are treated as owning part of the trust's real estate. Your share of the rent goes on your own return, and you deduct your share of the depreciation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Are you personally liable for a DST's loan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not in the trust the IRS ruling describes. The trust took over the property's loan, and neither the trust nor any owner is personally liable on it."}}]}</script>
